WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:19.860
In Season 5 of The Wire, Gus, a newspaper
editor says this.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.520
This is an ethos that The Wire takes to heart
and uses to develop it’s themes and ideas.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.690
As I laid out in part 1 of this series on
The Wire, show creators David Simon and Ed

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.660
Burns, had intentions for the show that went
far beyond creating entertainment.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.280
They were interested in “what feels true”
and they used moments to examine and develop

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.830
their themes.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.730
In this video I’m going to show how the
wire uses narrative structure, and the juxtaposition

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.720
and relationship of scenes and characters
to illustrate ideas.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.649
To do this, I’ll have to spoil aspects of
the show.

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:57.540
If you haven’t seen the show yet, and you’d
like a spoiler free overview of why it’s

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.420
great, make sure to watch part one in this
series.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:12.830
All in the Game: The Wire’s Message

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.350
The Wire deals with many issues, but it’s
primary focus is illustrating the city of

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:22.830
Baltimore by showing its various institutions,
and the individuals that make up those institutions.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:28.370
The show portrays how economic, political
and individual motivations affect how institutions

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.370
operate.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:34.570
Key to the story is how these motivations
keep the institutions in a cycle of failure,

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:45.440
and make it difficult for individuals to reform.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.970
But how do you lay these ideas out on film?

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:55.150
How do you portray them without beating your
audience over the head with preachy, complicated

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.490
exposition?

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:59.360
One way is to use the technique that Gus describes.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.650
You show individual moments, and use the relationship
between moments in different character’s

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.020
lives to infer certain ideas.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:11.340
Here’s an example, early in season 2 there
are two scenes that mirror each other visually

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.500
and in content.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:23.470
Stinger Bell and Avon Barksdale communicate
through the chainlink fence of a prison.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.860
Daniels and McNulty communicate through the
chainlink of the evidence room.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:37.890
The conversations in the two scenes are different
in how the relate to the story, but It illustrates

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.470
the dysfunction of the police department,
who is punishing the officers that successfully

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.959
arrested Avon Barksdale.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:48.520
None of this is in exposition.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.920
The Wire doesn’t tell you these two institutions
are similar, it just lets you see that they

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:52.920
are.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.709
The idea is simply established through context
and the visual of the two chain link fences.

00:02:54.709 --> 00:03:00.430
Through these kinds of associations the show
creates the City of Baltimore as a character.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.269
Each institution and character touches every
other in some way.

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:07.310
Shifting an aspect of one, affects the others,
the failure of one, makes the success of another

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.310
less likely.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:14.250
Corner kids will take money that’s being
given away, just like Clay Davis

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:22.849
Clay davis is told he shouldn’t flip and
needs to carry the charge against him, just

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:28.870
like Deangelo is told the same thing.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:34.659
Deangelo is scolded for being unloyal to his
family, just like Carver is scolded for being

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:42.220
unloyal to his police unit.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:48.090
Carver is also scolded for going outside the
chain of command, while Carcetti later thanks

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:53.269
Daniels for doing the same thing.

00:03:53.269 --> 00:04:05.260
Carcetti is trying to do politics differently
but struggles against the cons of Clay Davis,

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:19.590
just like Stringer Bell does when trying to
reform his drug empire.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:26.210
Stringer Bell is punished by his institution
for that very attempt to reform, just like

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:34.520
Bunny Colvin for his attempt to reform.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:41.219
Bunny Colvin reaches for a hairbrained illegal
scheme to try to force reform when all else

00:04:41.219 --> 00:04:49.140
fails, which is mirrored by McNulty’s hairbrained
illegals scheme to inspire change in season

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.509
5.

00:04:53.509 --> 00:05:03.199
McNulty’s actions in season 5 lead to him
losing what he loves, Police work, just like

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:11.770
Marlow’s mistakes lead to him losing what
he loves, his reputation.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:16.469
Even though Marlow tries to reform, he’s
drawn back to the streets, Just like Avon

00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:26.159
Barksdale was after an attempt to reform.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:35.099
Avon Barksdale is more interested in sending
a message than in effectiveness, just like

00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:42.379
Police Commissioner Burrell.

00:05:42.379 --> 00:05:48.550
This is just a fraction of the ways that characters
and moments are interconnected on The Wire.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:56.539
Doing this provides some of the context necessary
to seriously examine the issues.

00:05:56.539 --> 00:06:01.879
Separate individuals are also used to tell
aspects of a broader more general story.

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:05.129
A great example of this is Deangelo and Denis
Cutty.

00:06:05.129 --> 00:06:07.629
Deangelo is on a path to reform.

00:06:07.629 --> 00:06:11.530
But he’s killed before he has time to complete
this arc.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:16.580
Killing off Deangelo is necessary to illustrate
the forces that keep reform from happening.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.809
But what if he had made it out of prison alive.

00:06:19.809 --> 00:06:24.439
What would that have looked like?

00:06:24.439 --> 00:06:27.110
The Wire provides us with Denis Cutty.

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:31.270
Who illustrates what Deangelo’s path might
have looked like had he lived.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:35.650
There’s an amazing amount of nuance that’s
available with this type of storytelling.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:41.599
It’s one of the ways The Wire balances precariously
on the edge of cynicism and hope.

00:06:41.599 --> 00:06:44.069
Seeing Deangelo die is heartbreaking.

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:48.419
Getting to see Cutty successfully reform provides
a ray of hope.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:51.379
But even then, the struggles of reforming
are made very clear.

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:52.379
This technique of relating characters allows
the show to cover much more ground and give

00:06:52.379 --> 00:06:53.379
far more context than it would normally be
able to in only five linear seasons.

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:56.839
Another example of this are characters Jimmy
McNulty and Kima Greggs.

00:06:56.839 --> 00:07:01.309
We meet McNulty in season 1 as a talented
but dysfunctional detective.

00:07:01.309 --> 00:07:06.749
He has a drinking problem, has trouble keeping
relationships together, and is jaded by his

00:07:06.749 --> 00:07:07.749
career.

00:07:07.749 --> 00:07:15.389
Kima’s arc over the entire show, helps to
illustrate how Jimmy ended up how he was in

00:07:15.389 --> 00:07:16.389
Season 1.

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:21.039
By doing this The Wire tells the individual
stories of Jimmy McNulty and Kima Greggs,

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:27.159
but it’s also telling the broader story
of the “Jaded, dysfunctional detective.”

00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:32.699
It tells the individual story of Deangelo
Barksdale and Denis Cutty, but the broader

00:07:32.699 --> 00:07:34.550
story of “reformed gangster.”

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:40.679
It tells the individual story of Micheal and
Omar Little, but the broader story of “stickup

00:07:40.679 --> 00:07:41.679
man”

00:07:41.679 --> 00:07:48.089
Ultimately the story of the kids and seasons
4 and 5 are the largest piece of this inter-character

00:07:48.089 --> 00:07:49.159
narrative.

00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:54.100
By showing each kid’s struggles and what
leads them to their final destinations, we

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:58.739
see how characters like Omar, Bubbles and
Bunny Colvin might have come to be.

00:07:58.739 --> 00:08:04.869
It allows us to connect the failure of the
school system to the dysfunction of the institutions

00:08:04.869 --> 00:08:06.639
in season 1-3.

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:11.580
But the show isn’t so deterministic as to
say that the individual adhering to the broader

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.740
story is inevitable.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:19.550
Through great effort, some individuals can
break out of the cycle and behave differently.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:25.369
Unfortunately though, it often takes great
trauma to inspire an individual to reform.

00:08:25.369 --> 00:08:26.369
This is illustrated in Season 4 through Bubbles
and Nehman.

00:08:26.369 --> 00:08:27.369
Two characters that successfully reform.

00:08:27.369 --> 00:08:28.369
Their reform is inspired by painful events.

00:08:28.369 --> 00:08:31.550
It’s the Wire’s call for individual reform,
but also an insinuation that, similarly true

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:37.190
reform for institutions may only come through
trauma to the institution.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:41.879
And understanding this broader narrative about
institutions helps us understand why a character

00:08:41.879 --> 00:08:45.230
like Omar is so compelling in the show.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:50.990
In a world where so many characters are bound
or hobbled by the failing institutions around

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:58.670
them, a true rogue, a lone rebel, is someone
we can root for.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:04.240
But all this is left for the audience to extrapolate
for themselves from individual moments, which

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.990
keeps the show from feeling like it’s shoving
a message down your throat.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.790
There are layers to unravel, and depths to
examine and interpret.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:18.661
The show allows you to use your worldview
to see the issues it’s laying out, even

00:09:18.661 --> 00:09:29.960
as it might challenge your own assumptions
and worldview, and present its own.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:34.300
In the final part of this Series I’ll be
examining more layers of the onion, and showing

00:09:34.300 --> 00:10:32.579
why each season of the wire is important

00:10:32.579 --> 00:10:56.000
to the story.

